  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1245 PM MDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nThe ridge will settle over the Southern Plains Sunday and Monday, \nallowing for continued moisture transport into western New Mexico. \nScattered to numerous showers and storms are expected across the \nwestern half of the state during the afternoon and evening hours, \nwith PWATs remaining around or above 1 inch. While locally heavy \nrain and flash flooding will be the main hazards, faster storm \nmotions toward the northeast at 10-20 mph should help limit the \nduration of rainfall over any one location. However, repeated \nrounds over the same area could still produce flash flooding. \nOutflows from western convection may once again propagate into the\nRio Grande Valley late in the day, bringing periods of strong \nwind and an opportunity for isolated storms. Through Monday, the \npersistent ridging and lack of rainfall will keep eastern New \nMexico hot, with an increasing number of locations potentially \nreaching the triple digits.\n\nBeyond Monday, model guidance disagrees as to where the ridge \nwill set up. With the extended forecast reliant on the NBM\nsolution, which is leaning heavily similar to that of the ECMWF, \nthe consensus is that the ridge will be elongated and mainly \nfocused over the Southeastern CONUS. That being said, we are \nexpecting more of a transition to a less active monsoon pattern by\nTuesday. However, any lingering moisture should be sufficient \nenough to promote isolated to scattered high-terrain convection \neach afternoon through the end of the period, with locally heavy \nrainfall and gusty outflows still possible.",
